gic400.library/--background--                     gic400.library/--background--

   NAME
	--background-- -- the versions of the library, and how they are written here

   FUNCTION
	The calls of this library are described one by one below. This entry
	says since when each exists, and how a change is told, as the autodocs
	of the system do (see exec.library/AllocPooled or StackSwap).

	A call that was added after the first version of the library has its
	version at the end of the line of its NAME, like "(V1.10)": the number is
	the version of the library in which it appeared. A change in what a call
	does is written in its FUNCTION or NOTE text, "As of V1.3 ..." and "Prior
	to V1.3 ...". A call without a version in its NAME has been there since
	1.0.

   VERSIONS
	The history comes from the releases of the project (the tags v1.0 to
	v1.9) and from the release notes, RELEASE-NOTES.md.

	All 21 calls are in the first release, and none has been added or
	removed since. What changed in what they do is below.

	1.0   2025-10-27  First release.
	1.1   2026-04-03  All SPIs are unrouted from CPU 0 before the controller
	                  is enabled. The dispatcher returns another value when
	                  an interrupt out of range is acknowledged. No call
	                  changed.
	1.2   2026-04-10  Not released on its own. The global variables are gone:
	                  the state of the library is in its base.
	1.3   2026-05-05  The calls return a LONG with distinct negative codes,
	                  GIC400_ERR_* (see each call). Before, every failure
	                  returned -1, as a ULONG. The library can be put in a
	                  ROM.
	1.4   2026-06-16  The Resident priority is 126 instead of 0: it only
	                  changes the order in which the residents are set up.
	                  A check at build time keeps writable data out of the
	                  library. No call changed.
	1.5   2026-06-23  The debug output follows the backend chosen for the
	                  whole stack; built with -O3; the id string says
	                  major.minor. No call changed.
	1.6   2026-07-25  The debug output uses the TRACE tier. No call changed.
	1.7   2026-07-28  Builds with GCC 16.1. No call changed.
	1.8   2026-09-12  Can be built into a custom Kickstart ROM. No call
	                  changed.
	1.9   2026-10-06  The dispatcher handles every pending interrupt in one
	                  pass; a server may use A5 freely (see AddIntServerEx).

   NOTE
	lib_Version and lib_Revision of the library come from the same place as
	the id string, the version of the project: 1.9 gives lib_Version 1 and
	lib_Revision 9. This has been so since the first release, so lib_Revision
	tells what the library is.

	The error codes (GIC400_ERR_*, V1.3) are in libraries/gic400.h.

   SEE ALSO
	exec.library/OpenLibrary()

gic400.library/AddIntServerEx                     gic400.library/AddIntServerEx

   NAME
	AddIntServerEx -- register an interrupt server for a GIC-400 interrupt

   SYNOPSIS
	error = AddIntServerEx( irq, priority, edge, interrupt )
	d0                      d0   d1        d2    a1

	LONG AddIntServerEx(ULONG, UBYTE, BOOL, struct Interrupt *);

   FUNCTION
	Installs an Exec Interrupt structure as the server of one
	GIC-400 interrupt, configures the interrupt (priority, trigger
	mode) and enables it. The interrupt is routed to CPU 0 only.

	When the interrupt fires, the library's dispatcher (itself an
	EXTER server) acknowledges it, calls the server and signals end
	of interrupt. The server is entered with the Exec server
	conventions: A1 = is_Data, A5 = is_Code, A6 = SysBase, plus D0 =
	the interrupt ID. D0/D1/A0/A1/A5/A6 are scratch. The server's
	return value is ignored.

	Only one server per interrupt is supported; there is no
	chaining.

	Registering the same Interrupt structure twice for the same
	interrupt is not an error and does nothing.

   INPUTS
	irq             The interrupt ID. SPIs start at 32; the valid range is
	                0 up to the number of IDs reported by
	                GetControllerInfo() (maxIrqs, at most 1020).
	priority        GIC priority byte, 0 is the highest. In non-secure
	                mode the least significant bit reads as 0.
	edge            TRUE for an edge-triggered interrupt, FALSE for level-
	                triggered.
	interrupt       An initialised struct Interrupt; is_Code must not be
	                NULL. The structure must stay valid until
	                RemIntServerEx().

   RESULTS
	error           0 on success, GIC400_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT if interrupt
	                or its is_Code is NULL, GIC400_ERR_INVALID_IRQ if irq
	                is out of range, GIC400_ERR_ALREADY_REGISTERED if
	                another server already owns the interrupt.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/gic400.h>

	struct Library *GIC400_Base;

	if (GIC400_Base = OpenLibrary("gic400.library", 1))
	{
	    struct Interrupt isr;
	    LONG err;

	    isr.is_Node.ln_Type = NT_INTERRUPT;
	    isr.is_Node.ln_Name = "my isr";
	    isr.is_Data = (APTR)mydata;
	    isr.is_Code = (APTR)myserver;

	    err = AddIntServerEx(irq, 0, FALSE, &isr);
	    if (err == 0)
	    {
	        /* ... */
	        RemIntServerEx(irq, &isr);
	    }
	    CloseLibrary(GIC400_Base);
	}

   NOTE
	The interrupt is only signalled to the CPU if its priority is
	numerically lower than the priority mask, which the library
	initialises to 0x7F (see SetPriorityMask()).

	As of V1.3 a failure returns one of the distinct negative GIC400_ERR_*
	codes, as a LONG. Prior to V1.3 every failure returned -1, whatever the
	cause, and the result was a ULONG.

	As of V1.9 the dispatcher handles every pending interrupt in one pass,
	and a server may use A5 freely, as Exec allows. Prior to V1.9 each
	pending interrupt cost another level-6 exception, and A5 was not free
	for a server to use (the release notes of 1.9 list this as a fix).

   SEE ALSO
	gic400.library/RemIntServerEx(),
	gic400.library/SetPriorityMask()

