   SYNOPSIS
	priority = GetIntPriority( irq )
	d0                         d0

	LONG GetIntPriority(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Reads the priority byte of an interrupt.

   INPUTS
	irq             The interrupt ID. SPIs start at 32; the valid range is
	                0 up to the number of IDs reported by
	                GetControllerInfo() (maxIrqs, at most 1020).

   RESULTS
	priority        The priority byte (0 to 255), or a negative
	                GIC400_ERR_* code if irq is out of range.

   NOTE
	As of V1.3 a failure returns one of the distinct negative GIC400_ERR_*
	codes, as a LONG. Prior to V1.3 every failure returned -1, whatever the
	cause, and the result was a ULONG.

   SEE ALSO
	gic400.library/SetIntPriority()

gic400.library/GetIntStatus                         gic400.library/GetIntStatus

   NAME
	GetIntStatus -- read the pending, active and enabled state of an interrupt

   SYNOPSIS
	error = GetIntStatus( irq, pending, active, enabled )
	d0                    d0   a1       a2      a3

	LONG GetIntStatus(ULONG, BOOL *, BOOL *, BOOL *);

   FUNCTION
	Reads the state of an interrupt from the Distributor. Each
	output pointer is optional and may be NULL.

   INPUTS
	irq             The interrupt ID. SPIs start at 32; the valid range is
	                0 up to the number of IDs reported by
	                GetControllerInfo() (maxIrqs, at most 1020).
	pending         Receives TRUE if the interrupt is pending.
	active          Receives TRUE if the interrupt is active.
	enabled         Receives TRUE if the interrupt is enabled.

   RESULTS
	error           0 on success, GIC400_ERR_INVALID_IRQ if irq is out of
	                range.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/gic400.h>

	struct Library *GIC400_Base;

	if (GIC400_Base = OpenLibrary("gic400.library", 1))
	{
	    BOOL pending, active, enabled;

	    if (GetIntStatus(irq, &pending, &active, &enabled) == 0)
	        Printf("pending %ld active %ld enabled %ld\n", pending, active, enabled);
	    CloseLibrary(GIC400_Base);
	}

   NOTE
	As of V1.3 a failure returns one of the distinct negative GIC400_ERR_*
	codes, as a LONG. Prior to V1.3 every failure returned -1, whatever the
	cause, and the result was a ULONG.

   SEE ALSO
	gic400.library/EnableInt(), gic400.library/SetIntPending()

